From: Jordan Bettcher <thebettcher@gmail.com>
Subject: v8: ppc64 fuse the zero-extend and scale of an element index into rldic

ppc64 is in V8_TARGET_ARCH_RESTRICTIVE_LOAD_STORE
(v8/src/compiler/turboshaft/load-store-simplification-reducer.h:26), so its
kMaxElementSizeLog2 is 0 and every element access with a scaled index is
rewritten to

    WordPtrShiftLeft(index, element_size_log2)          (same file, :160, :195)

with the displacement folded in by a WordPtrAdd.  The index is a checked
Word32 that the graph builder widens with ChangeUint32ToUintPtr -- six sites
in turbolev-graph-builder.cc (:3327, :3360, :3367, :3936, :3953 among them),
and ChangeUint32ToUint64 in simplified-lowering.cc:2120 on the TurboFan path
-- which is ChangeUint32ToUint64 on a 64-bit target (assembler.h:2642).  So
a typed-array or FixedArray element load arrives at the backend as

    Word64Shl(ChangeUint32ToUint64(i), k)

and today the two nodes are selected separately:

    clrldi rT,ri,32        VisitChangeUint32ToUint64 -> kPPC_Uint32ToUint64
    sldi   rT,rT,k         VisitWord64Shl            -> kPPC_ShiftLeft64

rldic does both at once:

    rldic  rT,ri,k,32-k

VisitWord64Shl already matches Word64Shl(Word64BitwiseAnd(x, mask), k) and
emits kPPC_RotLeftAndClear64 when the shifted mask is a single contiguous
field.  ChangeUint32ToUint64(x) is Word64BitwiseAnd(x, 0xffffffff), so this
adds that match for 0 < k < 32, reading the ChangeOp's input directly.  The
operands follow the file's IsContiguousMask64 convention -- sh = k, mask =
31 + k, the index of the highest surviving bit -- and the existing lowering
in code-generator-ppc.cc

    rldic(out, in, sh, 63 - mask)

turns that into MB = 32 - k.  The code generator is not modified.

Correctness: rldic RA,RS,SH,MB is ROTL64(RS, SH) & MASK(MB, 63 - SH).  With
MB = 32 - k that keeps big-endian bits 32-k .. 63-k, i.e. little-endian bits
k .. 31+k.  For 0 < k < 32 the rotate carries x's bits 0..31 to k..31+k
without wrapping, and the mask clears x's bits 32..63 (now at 32+k..63 and
0..k-1), so the result is (x & 0xffffffff) << k for every x.  Verified on
POWER9 against that C expression for every k in 1..31 over random and edge
inputs.  k = 0 and k >= 32 stay on the generic path: for k >= 32 the
extension could be dropped altogether, as arm64 and loong64 do, but that is
a different rewrite.  No CanCover check is needed: if the ChangeOp has other
uses it is still emitted for them, so the fused sequence is never longer than
the current one.  arm64 fuses the same shape with ubfiz, and arm64, loong64,
mips64 and riscv64 all special-case Opmask::kChangeUint32ToUint64 as the left
input of a 64-bit shift.

rldic is a base 64-bit Book I instruction, present since the original PPC64
architecture and absent from the ISA 3.0 "V2.07 bit" table, and the existing
Word64BitwiseAnd match already emits it.  Valid for both the POWER8 (ISA
2.07) and POWER9 (ISA 3.0) pools; it adds no ISA 3.0 or 3.1 instruction.

Found by disassembling TurboFan output from the shipped build with
--perf-prof: 4 clrldi+sldi pairs per lin_solve iteration in Octane
NavierStokes and 3 per am3 iteration in Octane Crypto, one per element
access, and the pattern covers every typed-array and FixedArray access the
browser compiles.  Upstreamable as-is; not ppc64le packaging specific.

--- a/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/instruction-selector-ppc.cc
+++ b/v8/src/compiler/backend/ppc/instruction-selector-ppc.cc
@@ -820,6 +820,22 @@
   const ShiftOp& shl = this->Get(node).template Cast<ShiftOp>();
   const Operation& lhs = this->Get(shl.left());
   int64_t value;
+  if (lhs.Is<Opmask::kChangeUint32ToUint64>() &&
+      this->MatchSignedIntegralConstant(shl.right(), &value) &&
+      base::IsInRange(value, 1, 31)) {
+    // Word64Shl(ChangeUint32ToUint64(x), sh) is (x & 0xffffffff) << sh, the
+    // Word64BitwiseAnd case below with a 32-bit mask.  One rldic rotates
+    // left by sh and clears the high 32 - sh bits (mask bit 31 + sh in the
+    // IsContiguousMask64 convention, which the code generator turns into
+    // MB = 32 - sh), replacing the clrldi + sldi pair that visiting the two
+    // nodes separately produces.  Turboshaft's load-store simplification
+    // emits exactly this shape for every scaled element index.
+    int sh = value;
+    Emit(kPPC_RotLeftAndClear64, g.DefineAsRegister(node),
+         g.UseRegister(lhs.Cast<ChangeOp>().input()), g.TempImmediate(sh),
+         g.TempImmediate(31 + sh));
+    return;
+  }
   if (lhs.Is<Opmask::kWord64BitwiseAnd>() &&
       this->MatchSignedIntegralConstant(shl.right(), &value) &&
       base::IsInRange(value, 0, 63)) {
